    "story": "The sun is low enough to be a problem and high enough to be a long afternoon, and his hand is up because the glare has washed the whole horizon into one pale sheet and the thing he is looking for could be anywhere in it. The pack is heavy on his back and the rifle is heavy in his left hand, and the weight of both of them is the price of walking into ground nobody has cleared yet. He is not looking for the enemy — he is looking for the ground the enemy hasn't taken.",
    "notes": "This is the portrait the whole war set turns to when the shooting stops. One young soldier, hand up against the glare, rifle in the other hand, pack on his back, scanning a horizon that has gone pale and blank — and the horizon behind him has gone the color of old paper, which makes the looking itself the subject. It hangs best in a quiet room at eye level, because the man in it is looking past the person standing in front of him, and a collector who stands there a minute will find himself looking that way too. That is the frame to buy when you want the set to have a face on it.",
