    "story": "The pack has been on his shoulders long enough that he has stopped feeling it as a weight and started feeling it as a part of him, and the straps have cut two grooves into his shoulders that will still be there when the pack comes off. The rifle rides against the pack and the pack rides against his spine, and the road under his boots is dust and ruts and more dust. He keeps his head down because the ground is where his feet are, and his feet are the part of him that still knows where he is going.",
    "notes": "Some frames are about the battle, and this one is about the walk to it. A British soldier under a pack that has clearly been on his shoulders for hours, rifle riding against the strap, head bowed to the road, and the painterly treatment holds the whole scene in those warm, dusty tones that make the weight itself visible. It is the war set's most honest frame about what service actually felt like — not the charge, not the smoke, but the miles in between, and a collector who has ever carried a heavy pack over a long road will recognize the shoulders in this frame at once. Buy it for the pack, and for the shoulders under it — the march in this frame is the part of the war a wall remembers longest.",
