    "story": "The young ones read him the way they read the weather. If he's calm, the day is survivable; if he's scanning, it isn't. A man with that many mornings in his face has become the unit's barometer, and he knows it, and the knowing is its own duty — he has to wear calm like equipment, whatever the sky is doing. The orders, when they come, will arrive through a dozen channels. His face got there first. It has been delivering the real news for a long time now.",
    "notes": "There are portraits that flatter, and there are portraits that tell the truth, and this one tells the truth. An older Canadian soldier in a netted helmet and round glasses, the red patch on his shoulder and the pack on his back, looking off to the side with a mouth that has not smiled in a long while — and everything behind the face has been left pale and indistinct, so the face is the subject and the war is only the backdrop. It hangs best alone on a wall, because a face like this one does not share a wall well; it takes the room and holds it. Hang it alone, and let the face do the talking.",
