    "story": "An hour ago this was somebody's afternoon. Apples in a basket, a street that had never needed watching. Now the street is a sector, and the quiet is the loudest thing on it — because a village that knows every face can spot a wrong one faster than any lookout can, and the eyes he can't see, behind shutters and curtains, are doing their own watching. His hand stays up. The apples stay where they were. Between the two of them is the whole occupation.",
    "notes": "A warning is worth more than a charge. Two German soldiers as lookouts by a log wall, one on his feet with his hand up and the other sitting with his rifle, a basket of apples still on the ledge between them, and the warm, ordinary street around them makes a quiet moment feel like the loudest one in the war set. It belongs on a wall where the room is quiet enough to hear it, because the frame asks the person standing in front of it to stop and listen, the way the two men in it are doing. A collector will remember the hand in the air. He will not forget the apples on the ledge.",
