    "story": "The steel is cold against his knee and the sand is in his boots and the rifle is upright in his hands because the moment he sets it down he stops being a soldier and starts being a target. The hedgehog was built to stop a tank and it is stopping a tank, and the man behind it is small enough to hide behind it and tall enough to see over it, and both of those things matter right now. He is kneeling because standing gets you seen, and the beach is wide and the beach is open and the beach does not care how low you get.",
    "notes": "Some frames are about the man, and this one is about what the man is hiding behind. An American soldier kneeling behind a steel hedgehog — an obstacle built to stop a tank, borrowed for a morning by a man who needed it more — rifle upright, pack on, eyes on the beach beyond, and the steel sits in the sand like it has always been part of the beach. It belongs on a wall where the room is bright, because the light in this frame does the work: a man taking cover in the open, and the open refusing to give him any shade. Put it where the light is good, and let the steel do the talking.",
