    "story": "The sand is under them and the fire is over them, and the two men in front are grinning anyway — not because anything out here is funny, because a beach under fire has nothing funny in it, but because the grin keeps the fear from getting a hold of the rest of the face. The rifles are down in the sand and the hedgehogs are up all around them and the beach runs open in every direction, and nobody goes first alone. Somebody has to stand up and run, and the run starts with whoever stops lying down first.",
    "notes": "Hang this one where people have to walk past it slowly, because the grin is what stops them. Two men lying flat in the sand under fire, rifles down, hedgehogs all around them, and both of them grinning like the beach is a joke somebody told — a collection with this frame in it feels alive instead of grave, and the color holds it together, the pale sand and the khaki and the netted helmets all in the same soft light. A collector hangs it for the grins and finds, a month later, that he has been looking at the men behind them.",
