    "description": "A young American soldier in a netted steel helmet rises halfway out of a foxhole, his rifle held upright in one hand, his eyes fixed on the ground beyond the rim. The painterly color treatment holds the frame in warm, muted tones — the sand of the foxhole, the olive drab of his uniform, and the pale, cloudy sky all carrying the same soft light.\n\nPart of a broader body of painterly fine art photography, this World War II frame holds the foxhole minute, when a man is half in the ground and half out of it, watching ground that has not moved yet.\n\nThe rifle stays up, and the rim stays under his chest, and the ground beyond it stays quiet.\n\n© Wayne Heim",
