    "story": "Every wounded man asks the same first question, and it isn't about the war. How bad. The medic's whole trade is in how he answers it — hands first, voice low, nothing in his face that the man on the ground can spend. Around them the day is still loud with everybody else's war. His is two square feet of dirt and one man's knee under his hand, and he fights it the way other men fight whole hills: one honest minute at a time.",
    "notes": "A collector sees the red crosses first, and the hand on the knee second. The medic is down beside the wounded man with his bag still on his shoulder, checking before he does anything, and the tree behind them is doing what it can — the whole scene comes down to two men and a tree, and it holds a room the way a portrait holds a room. A collector hangs it where a gallery goes quiet, because the medic's minute deserves a quiet wall.",
