    "story": "He has already done the looking. Looking changed nothing, so now his hand goes to his forehead — the oldest ceremony there is, older than salutes: wipe the sweat, buy a second, stand in the heat with what you've seen. The wire behind him is still strung for men in a hurry. Nobody here is in a hurry anymore. The day will ask more of him before it's done; it always does. This minute, it is only asking him to stand in it.",
    "notes": "The sweat sells this one, because the war set is usually too proud to show it. The man is standing over a fallen comrade with his hand on his forehead, the wire and the stakes filling the ground behind him, and the frame has a man in it who has run out of things to do — the heat, the wire, the stillness, and a hand that goes to the brow because the brow is where it lands. A collector hangs it where a room feels too sure of itself, and lets the frame do the reminding.",
