    "story": "Nobody charges alone — that's the secret the bayonets are keeping. A line goes forward because courage, out here, is contagious in both directions, and every man in it is borrowing from the man beside him and lending back at the same time. Standing still was the unbearable part. This — dust in the teeth, the far side coming at them as fast as they can run at it — this is almost a relief. The charge isn't men unafraid. It's fear, finally given something to do.",
    "notes": "Every war set needs one frame with the whole line in it, and this is the set's panorama — a charge at full width, bayonets out, dust up, no hero singled out and no man going back. The brag is its honesty about how charges actually work: not one brave man, but a line of them lending each other the nerve. It takes a long wall and earns it; guests read it left to right like a sentence and arrive at the far side slightly out of breath.",
