    "story": "You are standing where the trouble stands. That's the whole design of a charge — it comes at the place you're standing, and the men in it have agreed, all of them, that your ground is worth more than their safety. The bazooka walks first because somewhere ahead there's a machine that has to be answered, and its carrier has the loneliest job on the beach: get close enough. The rest run the same errand at his shoulder. Whatever you do next, do it knowing they already have.",
    "notes": "Most charges in this set run across the frame. This one runs at you. That's the whole brag: the only head-on advance in the collection, bazooka first, close enough that a guest steps back from it — and a picture a guest steps back from is a picture that works every day it hangs. Collectors put it where the room needs a pulse, and it has never once been walked past.",
