    "story": "The steel was built for bigger game. A hedgehog doesn't care what it stops — a tank, a bullet, a man's whole afternoon — and the men behind it take the shelter in the spirit it was offered: crooked, partial, and better than the sand. Out front, the ground is playing the same game they are. Whoever moves first explains himself. So the steel holds still, the men hold still, and the beach waits to see which side gets tired of the arrangement.",
    "notes": "The brag in this one is the mismatch: steel engineered to stop a tank, spending its morning sheltering two men. Wayne Heim caught the war's great scale joke — the biggest hardware on the beach used as a garden wall — and collectors who know the beach obstacles spot it instantly and start telling the hedgehog story themselves. A frame that makes its owner sound knowledgeable is a frame that earns its wall.",
