    "story": "The sound gets there first. It always does — a tearing in the air that gives a man just enough time to understand what's coming and not enough time to do anything but receive it. The flinch isn't cowardice. The flinch is the body filing its honest report while the mind is still composing something braver. Hand on the steel. Mouth open. Every prayer he knows, arriving at once and out of order. Then the world will either resume, or it won't, and both outcomes are already decided somewhere above the wire.",
    "notes": "Every guest who has ever been startled recognizes this frame in their own body — the brace, the open mouth, the hand gripping steel — because Wayne Heim caught the one moment war photography usually misses: not the explosion, the hearing of it. The brag is simple and true: this is the set's portrait of sound. People lower their voices in front of it without knowing why.",
