    "story": "Ask a veteran what the war was and he will not say the battles. He will say the walking. This is the war at its most honest tempo — a sergeant's pace in front like a metronome, and behind him a line of men spending the day's strength at exactly the rate he sets, mile after mile of other people's countryside sliding past at three miles an hour. The rifles will matter later, somewhere up the road. Right now the most important weapon in the column is a steady pace, and the sergeant is carrying it for all of them.",
    "notes": "Hung next to its flagged companion, this is the march with nothing laid over it — and collectors who take both will tell you the plain one is the truer flag. The brag is its refusal: no charge, no smoke, just a sergeant's pace and a line of men spending a day at three miles an hour, which is what most of the war actually was. It is the frame veterans nod at. In a set of battles, that's the rarest endorsement there is.",
