    "story": "Sandbags at their backs — a wall that has already stopped something this morning. Protection. Or an obstacle. What he is shouting at is ahead of them, out where the wall does no good, and under fire a man shows what he's made of by what he tries to run toward. The hands on his arms have decided for him. Not yet. Not like this.",
    "notes": "Every other action frame in this set shows men going. This one shows them refusing to let one of their own go — a whole cluster moving as a single body around one man's shout, sandbags at their backs, under fire. That's the claim a collector can make in any room: it is the war's other half, the half about what men hold each other back from, and guests who have served recognize it before they can say why. It is the frame people describe to other people afterward.",
