    "story": "The water had a say in it. Cold through the boots before the boots were even wet. Diesel in the throat. Somewhere ahead, a voice calling names off a list — and the names answering, one by one, out of turn. He had written the letter in his head twice. Neither version survived the ramp. The man in front of him kept walking. He followed. That was the whole decision.",
    "notes": "This is the embarkation image collectors ask for first — the moment before the landing, not the landing itself. Heim stages it from behind, so the viewer boards with the men instead of watching them, and the crowded ramp gives the print a documentary weight the quieter portraits in the series don't carry. In a Facing History grouping it anchors the wall: the companion piece that explains why the portraits beside it matter, and the one guests linger over longest.",
