    "story": "The sea did the talking first. Salt on the lips. Engine thrum under the boots. Somewhere below, water slapping steel in a rhythm nobody had chosen. He had done the maths in his head — how long the crossing, how far the beach, how many names he still owed a letter to. The maths kept coming out wrong. So he counted something else. Breaths. Gulls. The seconds between one wave and the next. And kept his eyes on the horizon, because looking down was a different kind of drowning.",
    "notes": "Collectors gravitate to the portraits in this series that feel lived-in rather than staged, and this is one of the strongest examples — the helmet, the life vest, the weathered face, all of it earned by the crossing it implies rather than by any prop. In a Facing History grouping it supplies the human anchor the wider battle scenes need: the proof, beside the landings and the marches, that someone actually stood on that deck, and the print guests stop in front of longest.",
