    "story": "The arm went up. That was all. No shout carried over the engine. No order anyone could repeat back later. Just a raised arm, and a column of men doing the arithmetic of it — how far to the sand, how deep the water at the ramp's end, how long a man could hold his breath and his nerve at the same time. Somewhere in the middle of the line, somebody laughed. It came out wrong. Nobody corrected him. The sea kept slapping the hull. The sky kept its own counsel. And the ramp stayed down, open to the sea.",
    "notes": "This wide ramp image is the companion the close portraits in the series need — the one that shows the whole machine of a landing, sailors and soldiers together, instead of a single face. Collectors who own one of the embarkation portraits usually come back for this frame, because it turns a portrait wall into a scene: the scale, the weather, the signal. It is the print that makes visitors ask how it was made, and then stay to hear the answer.",
