    "story": "One finger. That was the order. Not a speech. Not a plan anybody could fold and put in a pocket. Just a raised finger, and the weight of it settling across a whole column. Behind him, boots shifted. Somebody swallowed. Somebody else thought of a kitchen table, a radio, a dog asleep under it — and then thought of nothing at all, because thinking had become a luxury. The wind came off the water smelling of fuel and salt. The sailor did not look back. He had already decided what his face would look like when the ramp dropped, and he was practising it now.",
    "notes": "This is the signal image of the embarkation group — the one frame where a single sailor, not the crowd, carries the moment. Collectors value it because it reads as a portrait of command without a portrait's stillness: the raised hand, the turned head, the column held behind him. Paired with the wider ramp scenes from the same landing, it gives a Facing History wall its pivot point, the frame everything else in the group seems to be answering.",
