    "story": "The hand said it before the mouth did. A circle of thumb and finger. All clear. Go. Behind him, the men exhaled in a way they would not have admitted to. The engines changed their note. The shore — the safe one, the one with tea and dry socks and a name they could pronounce — began to slide away, and nobody turned to watch it go. Fuel and cold sea filled his nose. Somewhere behind him a letter lay half-finished in a pack that was no longer his to open. Ahead of them the crossing went on, and everything after it, still unspent.",
    "notes": "Among the embarkation frames this is the most direct — a single hand doing all the talking, and a whole column of faces behind it doing the listening. Collectors tend to single it out, because the gesture reads across a room. In a Facing History grouping it works as the opening frame: the departure before the landing scenes, the moment the story actually starts moving.",
