    "story": "It was not a wave. It was not a greeting. A finger, raised against a grey sky, and the engine note changing underneath him. He could feel the hull answer through his boots. Somebody behind him was praying, quietly, in a language the wind kept stealing. Somebody else was counting the rivets on the side of the craft, because numbers were easier than names. The sea smelled of iron and fuel. The shore he had left was already a rumour. Ahead, only the beach — and the long minute before it.",
    "notes": "This is the most graphic frame of the embarkation group — almost abstract, a hand and two helmets against steel and sky, and all the more powerful for it. Collectors who like the series' painterly side reach for this one first: it reads as a poster and a photograph at once, and it gives a Facing History grouping its boldest, simplest image — the one a visitor can read from across the room and still be thinking about on the way home.",
