    "story": "A man could hold the rail. That was something. Not the orders. Not the map. The rail. Cold steel under the forearms, rope rough against a knuckle, the whole craft rolling just enough to remind a man he was on water. Nobody spoke about the shore. It was there in the set of every jaw, in the way nobody looked at anybody else for too long. Below deck, somebody was sick, quietly, into a bucket, and nobody turned around. The men above deck kept their eyes out. Wet wool and fuel hung in the air. Uninvited, a hymn his mother used to hum surfaced in the back of his mind and would not go back down. On the other side of the water, tomorrow sat unopened.",
    "notes": "This is the crowded-deck frame of the embarkation group, and it works because of its faces — a whole row of them, each one looking a different direction and thinking a different thought, held together by the steel rim and the rope. Collectors prize it for that chorus effect: it is not one portrait but a dozen, and it gives a Facing History wall its widest human register, the frame that turns a set of portraits into a company.",
