    "story": "The dirt did the talking. It came up in sheets, stinging the cheek, gritting the teeth, getting into the eyes before a man could blink it away. Somewhere to the left, a voice was shouting a name — his name, somebody else's, it hardly mattered now. The rifle kicked. The world narrowed to the next breath, the next round, the next few yards of mud that had to be crossed somehow. He thought of dry ground. He thought of a hand on his shoulder, long ago, telling him to keep his head down. He kept it down. And then, because there was nothing else to do with it, he stood his ground and fired again.",
    "notes": "This is the action frame the embarkation pictures have been building toward — the spray, the recoil, the mud, all of it in motion at once. Collectors who own the quiet crossing images need this one to complete the arc: without it, the story ends at the ramp. With it, the wall has its climax, the print that stops a visitor mid-sentence and makes the rest of the grouping land.",
