    "story": "They did not stop. That was the order nobody had given and everybody had understood. Dust in the mouth. Smoke in the eyes. The man on the ground — a name, a laugh, a card game the night before the crossing — already becoming a weight they would carry later, when there was time to carry anything. Now there was only the next few yards. The rifle kicked. Somebody shouted, and the shout tore itself ragged on the dust. The ground shook under another man's boots as he ran, and ran, and did not turn back. Because to stop was a place you could not come back from — not today.",
    "notes": "This is the hardest image in the series to look at, and the one collectors return to — a fallen man in the foreground and the advance continuing over him, the whole moral weight of the series in a single frame. It is not a comfortable print, and it is not meant to be. In a Facing History grouping it is the conscience of the wall: the frame that stops the landing story from becoming adventure, and reminds a visitor what the other pictures were for.",
