    "story": "He walked. That was the strange part. After all the noise, after the dirt in the teeth and the shouting that had torn itself ragged, he walked — one foot, then the other, through dust that hung in the air like a held breath. The rifle was heavy across his body. The man beside him had gone down to one knee and was aiming at something nobody else could see. Behind the bank, the others were still firing. He did not run. He did not duck. Somewhere in his head, a tune from a dance hall — a saxophone, a girl's hand on his arm — kept playing through the noise, out of place and out of time. He let it play. And he followed it forward.",
    "notes": "Where the previous frame in this sequence is the fight itself, this one is the step after it — a man walking upright through dust, unhurried, almost calm, with the fallen at the frame's edge. Collectors buy the pair together for exactly that contrast. On its own, it is the more painterly of the two: looser, dustier, the composition carried by a single walking figure, and the frame in the group that rewards a long look.",
