    "story": "Low. That was the first lesson, and it stayed. Low, with the sand against the knees and the rifle finding its own line. The spray at his shoulder was not his — it was the ground answering something he could not see. He breathed through his mouth, slow, because the nose was full of dust and cordite. Somewhere ahead, a voice he almost knew was shouting a direction, and he filed it away for the second after this one. There was a photograph in his pack, creased at the corners, a face he had promised to come back to. He had promised it, and the promise had weight. It sat there, under the pack straps, pressing him forward, one crouched step at a time.",
    "notes": "Paired with the walking frame of the same soldier, this one is the grit — the same man, now low, now aiming, the story turning from movement to intent. Collectors who take the pair get the full arc, and this frame on its own is the more dramatic of the two: a tight, painterly crouch, sand in the air, the composition all slope and shoulder. In a Facing History grouping it is the frame that adds tension to the wall, the one that makes the quiet portraits beside it feel earned.",
