    "story": "The trench was a world with a lid of sky. Sand under the boots, sand in the collar, sand in the food nobody was eating. A man sat with his back to the wall and tried to remember what quiet sounded like. Beside him, somebody laughed — a short, surprised laugh, the kind that comes out when the body forgets to be afraid for a second. Above, the sky kept its business to itself. Below, in the dark under the packs, a mouse moved, and a man who had seen too much that week watched it go and felt, absurdly, grateful for something small and alive. Sweat, wet wool and gun oil — the trench smelled of all three. In a breast pocket, a letter sat unread. Ahead of them lay the next hour, and the hour after that, and no way to know which one would matter.",
    "notes": "This is the trench frame of the series — the closest, most crowded image in the sequence, and the one that shows what the landing scenes were leading to: men living in a hole in the ground, together. Collectors value it for its faces, half a dozen of them, each one different, and for the way Heim lets the trench itself do the framing. In a Facing History grouping it is the anchor of the inland section, the print that makes the open-beach images feel like a prologue.",
