    "story": "He heard it before he saw it. That was the trick of the trench — sound arrived first, and the eyes had to catch up. A scrape of a boot. A voice, low, from the next bay. The rifle was already where it needed to be, across his body, a weight he no longer noticed. He thought of the man behind him, the one whose back filled the frame, and of the unspoken agreement between them: you watch left, I watch right — that was the agreement, and it needed no words. The mud smelled of rain and iron. Somewhere far above the trench lip, birds were still going about their business, as if the world had not changed its mind about them. He envied them, briefly, and then he let it go. There was a job. There was the next minute. There was the look to the right, held a beat longer than usual.",
    "notes": "This is the portrait of the green-beret sequence — the close, waist-up frame where the action stops and the man takes over. Collectors who own the wider trench and slope frames add this one as the face of the group: the look to the right, the rifle ready, the mud on the uniform. In a Facing History grouping it is the portrait that gives the action scenes their man, and the frame visitors remember by his expression rather than by its scene.",
