    "story": "The hands went up before the words did. That was the point. Hands, high, where everybody could see them, the rifle held flat above his head like an offering. The smoke stung. The trench smelled of wet earth and cordite. Somewhere behind him, a man was shouting in a language he almost understood, and another was laughing — not at him, not exactly, but with relief, the kind of laughter that escapes when a body realises it is still alive. He thought of a kitchen, a window, a woman's hand on a doorframe, and then he stopped thinking, because thinking was a luxury for later. Now there was only the next minute, and the hands, and the keeping of them up, and the hope that the men coming toward him could read what the hands were saying.",
    "notes": "This is the surrender frame of the series — the one image where the war stops being an advance and becomes a decision, made by one man with his hands in the air. Collectors value it because it is the rarest register in the series: not a portrait of resolve, but of relief and fear in the same breath. In a Facing History grouping it is the frame that gives the landing and trench pictures their ending, the one visitors ask about first and remember longest.",
