    "story": "The pack was his address now. Rope, netting, pouches, a tin cup that clinked with every step — everything he owned, and everything he needed, riding on his back. The trench narrowed. The men along the edges lay low, watching him pass, and he felt their eyes without turning. He did not turn. Turning was a luxury, and a risk. The air tasted of mud and rain. Somewhere ahead, a voice was giving orders in short bursts, and he let the rhythm of it set his pace. He thought of the weight, and then he stopped thinking of the weight, and thought instead of the ground under his boots — firm, then soft, then firm again — and of the simple, stubborn fact of going forward, one step at a time, because stopping was not an option the trench allowed.",
    "notes": "This is the back view of the green-beret sequence — the frame that turns the soldier into every soldier, because you cannot see his face, only what he carries. Collectors buy it for that very openness: it is the most universal image in the series, the one a visitor can step into. In a Facing History grouping it works as the quiet close to the advance, the print that follows the action frames and lets the wall breathe.",
