    "story": "The gap was only a few yards. That was the joke of it. A few yards of open mud, and it felt like a country. He took it at a half-run, rifle held tight, boots finding the firmer ground by feel. Behind him, the men in the trench were watching — not cheering, not calling out, just watching, because watching was the only help they could give from where they lay. The air smelled of wet earth and rain that had not quite fallen. Somewhere ahead, a bird called, once, and stopped. He thought of a field at home, a fence, a dog that used to run ahead of him and then look back, checking he was still there. He was coming. He kept his head down, and his feet moving, and the few yards closed, one step at a time.",
    "notes": "This is the wide, open-ground frame that closes the sequence — a whole trench line in one image, the central figure caught mid-step, the others spread across the mud like a map of the advance. Collectors value it as a group piece: it is the frame that shows the scale of the thing, and it gives a Facing History wall its landscape, the print that lets the portraits and the close trench scenes breathe inside a bigger picture.",
