    "story": "Mud, first. Always mud. It came up over the boot tops and stayed there, a second skin that no amount of scraping would shift. The rifle was slick in his hands. He kept his head down — not from fear, exactly, but because the sky had stopped offering anything worth looking at. Around him, the others held their places, low, patient, rifles ready, watching a stretch of ground nobody had claimed yet. Somewhere behind the haze, a voice called a name, and nobody answered it, and the silence that followed was worse than the call. He kept walking. The bank was only a few yards across. It took a lifetime.",
    "notes": "This is the trench-line frame that opens the inland sequence — a single walking figure high on the bank, the rest of the company low in the earth, the whole composition balanced between movement and stillness. Collectors who followed the advance scenes ashore will recognise the register at once, and this frame is the strongest of the group: the bowed head, the mud, the hazy light. In a Facing History grouping it is the image that carries the inland section, the print that makes the landing scenes feel like the beginning of something longer.",
