    "story": "Keep your head down. The words were not spoken. They did not need to be. They were in the way the men at the edges held themselves — low, still, rifles ready, their eyes doing the talking. He felt them on his back as he crossed. The rifle in his hands was heavier than it had been that morning, or maybe his arms were simply tired, or maybe it was the mud, clinging to everything, making every step a small argument with the ground. Behind the haze, the trees stood thin and grey. Ahead, the bank dropped away into ground he had not yet seen. He thought of the men who would cross after him, and of the way they would watch him the way he was being watched now — and he understood, suddenly, that crossing was not the hard part. Coming back was. He was not coming back. He kept going.",
    "notes": "Where the previous frame catches the same man mid-stride, this wider one gives him his audience — the company low in the earth, watching him cross. Collectors who own the first frame add this one because it changes the question the picture asks: not what he is doing, but who is watching him do it. In a Facing History grouping it is the more theatrical of the pair, the print that turns a single crossing into a shared one.",
