    "story": "The ground was still wet from the night's rain, and it gave under his boots with a soft, sucking sound that made every step feel borrowed. He did not slow down. Slowing down was a way of thinking about it, and thinking about it was a way of stopping. The rifle was a weight across his body, familiar as a second spine. Behind him, the others were finding their own pace — one man on one knee, sighting down a barrel; another half-crouched, half-running, already out of breath. He could hear his own breathing, ragged, and the thump of his pack against his back, and somewhere in the middle of it all, a tune from a wireless, a dance hall, a girl's laugh — the wrong music for this ground, and yet it carried him, step after step, when nothing else would. He ran on. The trees ahead were thin and grey. The mud was everywhere. And the next few yards were his, and his alone, to take.",
    "notes": "This is the running frame of the trench sequence — the one where the advance stops being a walk and becomes a sprint. Collectors prize it for the central figure's profile, mouth open, rifle ready, pack bouncing — a portrait of momentum. In a Facing History grouping it is the energy of the wall, the print that makes the slower, lower frames around it feel like the moments just before and just after this one.",
