    "story": "The rifle's bolt clicked, and the sound of it was small — smaller than the thump of his boots, smaller than his own breathing, smaller than the dust that rose off the mound with every step. He was in the air. That was the first thing his body understood, and the second was that the ground had dropped away behind him and the ground ahead had not yet arrived. The man beside him was in the air too, or close to it, and the two of them held the same line, rifles out, eyes forward, as if the air itself were a place a man could run across. Below, the fallen man lay in the earth, and the mound, and the crouched man behind it, and the beret at the edge — all of it held still, as if the whole trench were holding its breath for the landing. He landed. The jar of it went up through his ankles and into his teeth. He did not stop. The next mound was already ahead, and the one after that, and the ground kept coming, and he kept taking it, one leap at a time.",
