    "story": "Nobody shouted. That was the first surprise. The trench was too narrow for shouting, too close for orders, too loud in the head for anything but the thump of boots and the scrape of a rifle butt against the earth. He had his teeth bared — not a snarl, not quite, just the face a man makes when his whole body is doing one thing and his mind has not caught up yet. The man above him was coming down the mound, and the man at his side was going the other way, and the whole trench seemed to be moving in two directions at once. The air was thick with dust and sweat and the smell of turned earth. He thought of a pub, a dartboard, a hand of cards — ordinary things, held in the mind like a lifeline — and then the thought was gone, knocked out of him by the next shove, the next step, the next breath. He held on. The trench held on. And somewhere, beyond the mound, the rest of the war was still going on without them, as if it had forgotten they were here.",
