    "story": "Don't drop it. The thought came first, before the hands, before the step, before anything else. A rolled bandage, white against the brown earth, turning end over end in the air, and his whole body answering it — arms out, fingers spread, the pack pulling at his shoulders, the mud sucking at his boots. Somebody had thrown it. Somebody, somewhere behind the mound, needed it, and needed it now, and the throw was the whole of the message. He could hear his own breath, loud in his ears, and the distant thump of something falling, and the voice in his head counting the seconds between the throw and the catch. The bandage spun. The trench narrowed to the space between his hands. He caught it — or he did not, and the earth took it, and the next throw would have to be better. Either way, the reaching was the story, and the reaching was already done.",
    "notes": "This is the most human frame of the trench sequence — not a rifle, not a charge, but a pair of outstretched hands and a bandage in mid-air. Collectors love it because it shows the war as aid rather than attack: the catch, the throw, the grimace. In a Facing History grouping it is the frame that stops visitors in their tracks, the print that turns a wall of battle into a story about looking after each other.",
