    "story": "It began with a hiss. Not a bang, not a crack — a hiss, soft and white, curling out of the canister in his hand and rolling across the mound like a slow tide. The smoke got into his eyes first, then his throat, then his clothes, until the whole trench smelled of it. He held his arm out straight, away from his body, as if the smoke were something he was offering the sky. Behind him, the other man was doing the same, and the two of them made a screen of it — a white wall, a blind, a way for the next men to move without being seen. He could hear boots, somewhere beyond the smoke, and the low voice of a man giving directions he could not quite make out. His face was set, teeth bared against the sting, and he thought of a bonfire, an autumn evening, a hand warming — and then the thought was gone, swallowed by the hiss. The smoke kept coming. The mound kept holding. And the men beyond it, unseen, kept moving, because the white wall said they could.",
    "notes": "This is the smoke frame of the series — the most photographic image in the sequence, white smoke against brown earth, a green beret in the middle of it, the whole composition made of contrast. Collectors value it because it is the one frame that needs no action to be dramatic: the smoke does the work. In a Facing History grouping it is the print that gives the wall its atmosphere, the image visitors remember by its colour alone — white, brown, and the face in the middle of it.",
