    "story": "Low was a discipline, not a posture. Anybody could crouch for a photograph of a second; staying low across a whole mound, with the rifle upright and the pack riding high and every instinct arguing for one quick look over the top — that was the discipline. The man behind the mound was prone and watching, and the watching was a kind of covering fire all by itself: it meant the stretch of ground had two sets of eyes on it and only one man exposed on it. He chose his next handhold the way a man chooses words in an argument he cannot afford to lose. The ground gave a little, took his weight, gave a little more. Below the crest, the war was a rumour in the next field. Up here it was knees, knuckles, and the slow arithmetic of not being seen.",
    "notes": "This is the careful frame of the green-beret sequence — no running, no leaping, just a man moving low over a mound with his rifle upright, the whole advance reduced to a single careful step. Collectors who own the louder frames add this one because it is the most painterly of the set: the grimace, the rope, the mound, the light. In a Facing History grouping it is the quiet centre of the trench section, the print that makes the action around it feel earned.",
