    "story": "You would have grinned too. Not because it was funny. Because the smoke had thinned for a second, and the trench had gone quiet for a second, and the whole war had shrunk, for one second, to a rifle held upright and a man crouched low in the earth, and the absurd, stubborn fact of still being alive. The grin was not for the camera. It was for the man beside him, the one whose rifle crossed the bottom of the frame, the one who had just said something — a joke, a curse, a name — that nobody would remember later and everybody would remember the feeling of. The pack dug into his shoulders. The rifle was slick in his hands. The smoke curled around the mound like a slow river. He held the grin, and the crouch, and the rifle, and thought of the garden gate at home, and the latch he had been meaning to fix since Easter — and then the smoke thickened again, and the second was over, and the trench was the trench again. But the grin stayed, somewhere under it all, like a light left on in an empty room.",
    "notes": "This is the grin of the series — the one portrait in the trench sequence where the soldier looks straight back at the viewer and smiles through the smoke. Collectors love it because it is the most human image in the group: no charge, no fall, no surrender, just a man crouched low, holding his rifle, and grinning. In a Facing History grouping it is the frame that breaks the tension of the wall, the print visitors warm to first and keep coming back to.",
