    "story": "The patch said Hampshire. He wore it the way a man wears a place name — not as a boast, but as a fact, the county he had left behind with its lanes and its chalk hills and a pub with a sign he could draw from memory. The helmet's net pressed against his hair. The chin strap cut a line across his cheek. He was looking at something off to the right, and whatever it was, it had his whole attention — not the camera, not the man behind him, not the light. He thought of the chalk hills above the village, and the lane that ran under them, and a dog that used to run the hedge line ahead of him — the ordinary things, held close, like a compass. The man behind him shifted his weight. The light changed, a fraction. He did not turn. Turning would have meant leaving the thought, and the thought was the ground he could stand on for a minute and still be a man from Hampshire, and not a soldier in a line.",
    "notes": "This is the portrait of the series — a single young face in profile, the Hampshire patch legible, the netted helmet, the second man soft behind him. Collectors buy it because it is the most collectible kind of image in the series: a face, a name of a place, and nothing else to distract from either. In a Facing History grouping it is the portrait that anchors the wall, the print guests ask about first — who he is, where he is from, and whether he came home.",
