    "story": "How long had he been lying there? Long enough for the grass to press its pattern into his forearm. Long enough for the rifle to stop feeling like a weight and start feeling like a part of him. Long enough for the man behind him to stop shifting and start breathing in time with him, slow, through the nose, the way the sergeant had taught them on a wet field that already felt like another life. The bayonet caught the light. He did not move it. Moving it would have meant thinking about it, and thinking about it would have meant thinking about what it was for. The commando patch on his sleeve said No. 4, and he thought of the number, and of the men who wore it beside him, and of the way a number can hold a whole company together when nothing else will. The grass swayed. The sky stayed pale. He stayed prone, and the ground stayed under him, and the next minute — whatever it held — would find him already there.",
