    "story": "The light was late afternoon, and it was lying. It made the field look gentle, the mound look soft, the rifles look like walking sticks, and none of it was true. The man in the middle knew it. His glasses had fogged, and he had wiped them, and they had fogged again, and somewhere in the middle of it he had stopped trying to see clearly and started trying to see enough. The men on either side of him stood the way men stand when they are ready to move and also ready to stay — rifles upright, weight even, eyes on the same stretch of ground. He thought of a pair of glasses on a bedside table, at home, and of the way the world looked without them — soft, and kind, and out of focus — and he wished, for a second, that the world could stay that way. It could not. The light changed. The field stayed a field. And the three of them stayed where they were, rifles ready, because the pause was a promise the day had made, and it was already being kept.",
    "notes": "This is the trio portrait of the series — three men, one field, the central figure kneeling with glasses and a rifle, the others standing guard on either side. Collectors value it for its balance: it is a group portrait and a landscape at once, and the glasses give it a face to remember. In a Facing History grouping it is the frame that turns a set of single portraits into a company, the print that makes the wall feel inhabited.",
