    "story": "That's the sea. He said it out loud, and then he wished he had not, because saying it made it real, and real was a weight he had not planned on carrying. The water was grey-green and flat, and it went on further than his eyes could follow, and somewhere in the middle of it the beach was waiting — a strip of sand, a few yards wide, and everything after it. The man beside him leaned on the ramp and stretched his arm out, as if he could touch the distance and make it smaller. The packs were heavy. The helmets were heavy. The air smelled of salt, and under the salt, the iron of the craft itself. A kitchen surfaced in him — kettle, morning light, the ordinary geography of a room where nothing was trying to kill him — and the sea took it back the way the sea took everything that morning: without comment. The ramp was still down. The next step was the water.",
