    "story": "The five on his helmet was a joke. Somebody had painted it on — a red diamond, a five, a mark that meant something to somebody, and the joke was that nobody could remember what. He wore it anyway, the way a man wears a nickname he did not choose. The rail was cold under his forearms. The sea stretched out, grey and flat, and the sky above it hung low and dark, and the two of them together made a ceiling and a floor with no walls at all. He thought of a field, a fence, a dog — no, he had already thought of the dog, and the dog was gone, and the thought was gone with it. What was left was the rail, and the men beside him, and the weight of the packs, and the long, slow roll of the craft under his feet. He leaned further. The horizon did not get any closer. It never did. He leaned anyway, because leaning was what there was to do, and the sea, for once, was listening.",
