    "story": "He counted the men. Not because he needed to. Because counting was the way his mind stayed in the room, or the craft, or whatever this was — a steel deck, a rail, a sea that stretched further than the eye could follow. Six men, seven, eight, and then he lost count, and the losing of it was a relief. The rail was warm from the sun. The helmets were close together, shoulder to shoulder, a wall of khaki and steel, and the man in front of him had a red diamond on his helmet that made him easy to follow with the eye. He thought of a queue, a bus stop, a morning like any other — and the thought thinned and went. The craft was still moving, and the horizon was still a line that refused to get any closer. The air tasted of salt. The engines hummed under his boots. He leaned on the rail, and the rail held, and the men held, and the whole crowded deck held together, one breath at a time, until the beach.",
