    "story": "A hand. Just a hand, resting on a helmet, and the whole of the crossing in it. The fingers were spread wide, as if the helmet were a head, and the head were a person, and the person were somebody worth holding on to. Under his palm the red patch said five. Five was a number you could follow in the water. Five was a man you could find again. He did not press down. He did not let go. The hand stayed where it was, warm through the steel, and the man underneath it kept his head still, as if the weight of it were what was keeping him upright. Behind them the engine note dropped, half a beat, and every man on the deck felt the crossing change under his boots. He kept his hand where it was. The helmet kept its number. And the crossing kept moving, one man at a time, toward a shore that had not yet shown itself.",
    "notes": "The closest crop in the landing sequence is also the quietest — a hand, a helmet, a red five, and nothing else to look at. Collectors who own the wider rail and deck frames come back for this one because it changes the scale of the story from a crowd to a touch. In a Facing History grouping it is the print that rewards the closest look, the image visitors lean into before they lean back, and the one that makes the whole embarkation group feel like it was made by a human hand.",
