    "story": "The cheer went up before the helmets did. That was the order of it — a sound, first, raw and ragged, and then the helmets, flying up into the pale sky, and the laughter that followed them, and the men who caught them again and threw them again because once was not enough. The beach was still a beach: sand, and obstacles, and a path of duckboards, and a lone man walking it as if the whole world had not just changed its mind. But the men at the front of the frame did not see him. They saw each other. They saw the sky. They saw the helmets, coming down again, and the hands, going up to meet them. The air was cold and damp, and the sea was grey, and none of it mattered for a minute, because the minute was theirs, and the helmets were theirs, and the beach — for the first time that day — was theirs too. He kept his helmet up. The others kept theirs up. And the cheer kept going, longer than any of them had planned, because nobody wanted to be the first to stop.",
    "notes": "Collectors who own the quiet crossing frames will want this one for the opposite reason — it is the loudest image in the series, and the happiest. A helmet in the air, a young man laughing, a whole beach of men doing the same: it is the celebration the landing pictures have been building toward, and the print that gives a Facing History grouping its lift. Visitors do not stand before it quietly. They smile first, and then they lean in, and then they ask what happened next.",
