    "story": "The path had been a lane once. You could tell by the way the grass grew at its edges, and the way the trees leaned over it, and the way the dust held the shape of feet long after the feet had gone. The men walked it the way men walk a lane they do not know — shoulder to shoulder, packs heavy, rifles slung, their boots finding the firmer ground by feel. The pack ahead of him was stencilled twenty-eight, crossed rifles under the number. He never did learn the man's name. The number was enough — it had been enough at every halt and every crossroads since the beach. The air was warm and green. The trees threw shade in long strips across the sand. He kept walking. The dust rose. The trees closed in. And the path went on, one step at a time, toward a place none of them had seen yet.",
    "notes": "Paired with the portraits that face the viewer, this is the frame that turns its back — and it is stronger for it. A whole company walking away down a sandy lane, dust at their boots, the pack marked twenty-eight: collectors love it because it is the most cinematic image in the sequence, a march rather than a moment. In a Facing History grouping it is the print that gives the wall its depth, the image visitors follow with their eyes down the path, wondering where it ends.",
