    "story": "The glasses came up. Came down. Came up again. Nothing moved in the valley that had not been moving an hour ago — a cow, too far off to matter, working the same strip of grass. Behind him the wall's stones held the day's warmth through his smock. The watch on his wrist said what it said. It changed nothing. Watching was the job: not the shot, not the advance, just the long, unpaid attention of a man paid in minutes. Somewhere below, a door closed in a farmhouse that had emptied at dawn. He marked it. Marked the road. Marked the treeline. When the word came, it would come because somebody had sat still long enough to earn it. Until then — glasses up. Glasses down.",
    "notes": "A portrait of watching, not of fighting — and that is exactly why collectors come back to it. The winged beret badge, the camouflage smock, the binoculars in both hands: it is the most specific portrait in the sequence, a man whose job is to see. In a Facing History grouping it is the frame that slows the wall down, the print visitors study longest, looking where he is looking and trying to see what he sees.",
