    "story": "Nobody had told him to pick it up. It sat where the wall's shadow ended, dark, heavier than its size had any right to be, and now it was in both his hands and the morning had narrowed to the width of it. Shake it? He would not shake it. Men he knew had shaken things. He turned it once, slow, the way you turn a stone you mean to skip — feeling for the side that wanted the light. Behind him, boots shifted on gravel. Somebody said his name, half a question. He did not answer. Whatever the box was, it had outlasted the house it came out of, and for one more minute it was his to not know.",
    "notes": "Collectors who collect the series for its faces will find this one the most arresting — a German soldier looking up, a small box in both hands, and a stone wall behind him. The goggles, the grey uniform, the rifle slung on the back: it is a portrait of a man caught in the act of looking, and the looking does the work. In a Facing History grouping it is the frame that stops the wall of action and asks the viewer to look with him, the print guests point at first and ask about longest.",
