    "story": "The jeep's engine ticked as it cooled. That was the loudest thing on the road, and he listened past it the way you listen past your own heart. The older man in the driver's seat had stopped pretending to read the map an hour ago. Now he just sat, hands loose on the wheel, watching the bend where the road came out of the trees — the bend everything would come around, friend or otherwise. The Sten's weight pulled at his arms. He let it. Arms that ache hold a gun steadier than arms that don't, somebody had told him once, in a kitchen, in another life. The bend stayed empty. The engine ticked. Nobody wound a watch.",
    "notes": "The jeep does half the work in this frame — its number, its wheel, its driver — and the man with the Sten does the rest. Collectors who own the portraits will want this one for the scene: a resistance fighter crouched by a jeep, the whole image held in a single crouch. In a Facing History grouping it is the print that gives the wall its vehicle, the image visitors point at first, reading the number on the jeep and then the face of the man beside it.",
