    "story": "The wall was the only shade for a hundred yards, and he had it, and it was not enough. A man gets a wall like this the way he gets a rumour — briefly, and already owing it to somebody else. His rifle stood upright between his hands, barrel to the sky, and he rested his forehead the way other men rest theirs in church. What was he waiting for? Orders. Relief. The war to remember his name. The lane stayed empty. A fly worked the rim of his helmet and he let it, conserving everything — movement, hope, the last of the water. When he lifted his head again, the light would have moved. That was the only promise the day had made him.",
    "notes": "The tight crop is the story here — the wall, the rifle, the crouch, nothing else for the eye to land on. Collectors who own the airborne and resistance portraits will want this one for the other side of the frame: a young German soldier waiting in silence, every line of the composition holding him in place. In a Facing History grouping it hangs as the quiet counterpart to the helmets-in-the-air scenes, the print a visitor studies longest, reading the camouflage and the bowed head and the rifle held upright like the last straight thing in the morning.",
