    "story": "\"Stay with me.\" He said it to the man on the ground, and then he shouted it up the lane for the medics, and the two sayings were the same saying. His hand was on the helmet because the helmet was where a hand could do its work — hold a head still, hold a man in the world. The rifle stood upright in his other fist, a flag nobody was saluting. Grass smell. Cordite, older, from up the hill. His knees were going dead in the wet grass and he did not shift them; shifting felt like leaving. \"Stay with me.\" The lane answered with boots, far off, coming at the pace boots come when the message has finally got through.",
    "notes": "Collectors who own the quiet frames will want this one for the shout — the hardest field in the series to sit with, and the one a visitor never walks past. The kneeling man, the upright rifle, the fallen comrade in the grass: a composition that holds its drama in a single gesture, the hand on the helmet. In a Facing History grouping it hangs as the image that gives the wall its weight, the print collectors point to when they talk about why the series matters — not the battle, but the staying.",
