    "story": "There. His arm did the talking — out flat, fingers together, the way they'd all been taught in a field in England that smelled of rain instead of this. The prone man read the arm the way you read a signpost: past the wall, left of the lone tree, the fold in the grass where the ground dips. Nobody looked at the man lying behind them. Looking was for after. The pointer's shoulder burned. He kept the arm up anyway, because an arm that drops mid-order takes the whole line's nerve down with it. Grass ticked against his sleeve. Somewhere a lark was doing its whole careless performance. There. Stay on it. There.",
    "notes": "The gesture is the composition — the pointing hand, the prone rifle, the fallen man, all of it held in one line across the grass. Collectors who own the stone-wall casualty frame will want this one for the movement: the same ground, a moment earlier, the advance still moving. In a Facing History grouping it hangs as the image that gives the wall its story, the print a visitor studies longest, reading the gesture and the rifle and the man lying behind.",
