    "story": "Through the sights, the world was a notch and a blade and a strip of bright grass. Breathe out and the blade settled. Breathe in and it wandered, and a wandering blade tells lies. So he made himself a miser with air — half a breath, held, the grass cold through his elbows, a stone under his ribs that he had decided, twenty minutes ago, to call furniture. Behind him the pointing went on; he could feel it the way you feel weather coming. His shot, when the word reached him, would not be the first one of the morning. It would be the one that counted. The notch. The blade. Half a breath, spent slow.",
    "notes": "Paired with the advance frame that points the way, this is the frame that lies down — the same ground, the same men, the rifle in the grass. A collector who takes the pointing frame will want this one beside it: the advance seen from the prone position, the composition tightening around a single rifleman. In a Facing History grouping it hangs as the image that gives the wall its distance, the print a visitor studies longest, reading the rifle and the pointing hand and the man lying behind.",
