    "story": "Wood against his cheek, warm as a hand. The Thompson had been carried by somebody else before him — the stock held a shine where another man's jaw had worried it — and he had made his peace with inheriting it. Crates at his back, lane in front, and the whole of his education narrowed to a front sight and patience. You don't fire a Thompson to hit one thing. You fire it to convince a whole lane. So the lane, for now, stayed unconvinced and empty, and he stayed a student of it: light, dust, the exact sound the wind made in the gap between the crates. His finger rested alongside the trigger, where resting was still allowed. The lane kept its lesson going.",
    "notes": "Collectors who own the quiet frames will want this one for the weapon — the Thompson, held the way a man holds a thing that has already saved him once. The cheek to the stock, the foregrip, the sling hanging loose: a composition that narrows the whole of the war to a single gun and a single man. In a Facing History grouping it hangs as the image that gives the wall its purpose, the print a visitor studies longest, reading the drum and the stock and the face pressed to both.",
