    "story": "She weighed less than his pack. That was the shameful arithmetic that came first, before the gratitude, before anything noble: less than the pack, less than the ammunition, less than the water he'd drunk that morning. Her arms locked around his neck with the strength left over from fear, and her hair, come loose, stuck to the sweat on his face. He did not know her name. She did not know his. The road to the aid station was a rumour other men had pointed at. So he walked toward the pointing, one foot, the next foot, and made her a promise he said out loud so the road could hear it too: \"Not setting you down.\"",
    "notes": "Collectors who own the quiet frames will want this one for the weight — the only carry in the series, and the image that gives the whole sequence its heart. The soldier's strain, the woman's clinging arms, the patch on his sleeve: a composition that holds its drama in a single back, bent under a burden. In a Facing History grouping it hangs as the image visitors ask about first — who she was, why he carried her, where the road led — and the print never answers, which is why it stays.",
