    "story": "There. A finger, out flat, and the whole field rearranged itself around it. The sergeant followed the arm. The others followed the sergeant. Behind them, the dust settled on shoulders that had stopped noticing dust. The man pointing did not shout. Shouting was for later, for the open ground, for the moment the finger became a trigger. Right now the finger was enough. It did the map's work in this light. Over there. Past the dead grass. Where the ground folds. The sergeant nodded, once, and the line began to move. And the pointing man said it, quiet, like a man naming a street he had walked as a boy: 'There.'",
    "notes": "The finger is the composition — one arm out flat, four men behind it, the advance held in a single gesture. Collectors who own the quiet crossing frames will want this one for the gesture: a sergeant, a point, a line of men in dust. In a Facing History grouping it hangs as the image that gives the wall its direction — a visitor's finger finds the pointing man first, every time.",
