    "story": "Keep your head down. Nobody said it out loud. The dust made the point, a mouthful at a time, and the tube made it with every step, banging against his hip. The tube rode across his chest like a second spine, and the second spine had a name he did not say out loud. Behind him, the column bent lower with every yard, the way wheat bends before a storm. The glasses kept slipping. He pushed them back with two fingers and read the ground the way other men read newspapers, one line at a time. Somebody behind him coughed. The cough was swallowed. The dust was in his teeth, and his mouth had forgotten what water tasted like. He raised his hand to signal the halt, and his hand was still rising when the dust closed over them.",
    "notes": "Collectors who own the quiet frames will want this one for the weight — a bazooka across a chest, a column bent low, the dust doing the talking. The glasses, the tab patch, the tube: a composition that narrows the advance to a single man and the men bent behind him. In a Facing History grouping it hangs as the image that gives the wall its burden — the one a visitor points at and says, that tube, that dust, that man.",
