    "story": "The grass hissed. That was the first sound, and it stayed in his boots long after the field went quiet. The man beside him ran with his rifle held the way a carpenter holds a saw, and the two of them cut a line across the green that had not been there an hour before. He had counted nineteen steps since the hedge. Counting had become a habit, the way habits take hold when the ground is open and the sky is too big. At the last halt, his friend had said the field would be the easy part. The field disagreed, politely, with every step. His rifle was heavy. His pack was heavier. And the green, bright as it was, gave him nothing to hide behind. He ran anyway. The grass hissed. And then — nothing. No sound at all, anywhere, for one whole breath.",
    "notes": "Paired with the dust-advance frames, this is the green one — the same run, different ground, the grass under their boots bright as a lawn. Two soldiers, rifles ready: a composition that holds its motion in a pair. In a Facing History grouping it hangs as the image that gives the wall its colour — the one a visitor points at and says, that grass, that run, those two.",
