    "story": "How heavy was the pack? Heavy enough to change the way a man ran. Heavy enough that his back knew it before his legs did. The man in front had stopped feeling his an hour ago. The man behind still felt every ounce, and the feeling kept him honest. The rifle with the tube on its muzzle was not his. He had picked it up off the ground at the hedge. It was the one choice of the morning he did not regret. The field was familiar. Familiar was not the same as safe, and safe was not a word anyone used out here. He watched the pack ahead of him bounce, and the bouncing had a rhythm he recognised, from another field, in another year, when the pack had been a schoolbag and the road had been a lane. The man in front turned his head, just enough. Their eyes met. Neither of them smiled.",
    "notes": "The tube on the rifle is the composition — a long muzzle, a pair of runners, the image held in a single stride. Collectors who take the grass-field frame will want this one beside it: the same two men, the same green, a moment later, the grenade launcher now in plain sight. In a Facing History grouping it hangs as the image that gives the wall its pair — visitors read the tube first, then the packs, then the two men running.",
