    "story": "The pack slapped his back with every step. Slap, slap, slap, a rhythm the column kept without agreeing to it. He was third in line, and third in line was the best place to be: close enough to see the shoulders ahead, far enough that the dust belonged to somebody else. The man in front of him carried his rifle the way a man carries a ladder, up over one shoulder, and the ladder swung with his stride. The field was green, a green he had not seen in weeks, and the weeks had been all dust and stone. He wanted to stop. He wanted to sit down in the grass and let the column go on without him. He did not stop. He did not sit down. The pack slapped his back. Slap, slap, slap. And somewhere ahead, a voice — not a shout, a voice — said the word they had all been waiting for.",
    "notes": "After the pair frames, this is the wide one — four men, the column, the grass field from behind. The packs, the rifles, the grass bright under their boots: a composition that holds its motion in a line. In a Facing History grouping it sits beside the pair frames and finishes the thought: the column, not the men, is the subject.",
