    "story": "He did not shout to be heard. He shouted because the field had gone quiet in a way that frightened him. The quiet was worse than the shelling. The shelling had a rhythm. The quiet had none. His throat was raw. It had been raw since the morning, and the morning had been all walking and waiting. The canteen in his hand was full. He had not drunk from it. Drinking was for after, and after had not come yet. The pouch across his chest held the things he could not afford to lose. He did not know what was in it anymore. He had stopped checking. The men behind him were listening. He could feel the listening, a pressure at his back, and the pressure kept his voice up. The canteen swung from his hand. It swung, and swung, and did not stop.",
    "notes": "The open mouth is the composition — a shout caught mid-word, the canteen in the hand, the pouch across the chest. Collectors who own the quiet portraits will want this one for the volume: a sergeant's voice, the frame leaning into it, the canteen swinging in the hand. In a Facing History grouping it hangs as the image a visitor hears before they see — step close, and the shout is still there.",
