    "story": "The pole was longer than he was. He carried it on his left shoulder. His right hand held his rifle. The rifle hung from its sling. The sling bit his shoulder. He did not shift it. He had not set the pole down since the breach. The officers called it the breach. They meant the wire. The wire was somewhere ahead of him, under the dust. He could not see it. To his left, the prone man fired in short bursts. The bursts came steady. His feet moved with the bursts. The pole stayed up.",
    "notes": "The pole is the composition — a vertical line in a field of horizontals, the sandbags, the sky, the trees. It answers a question the sandbag frames only raise — why one man stands when the others are down. In a Facing History grouping it hangs where a visitor stops to ask what the pole was for.",
