    "story": "The pack pulled him backward and the rifle pulled him forward. Between the two pulls, the berm took what weight it could. The distance to the treeline, judged by eye, then judged again — the eye disagreed with itself, and that was the point of judging twice. The berm smelled of turned earth and wet leaves, and under that, faintly, the oil on the bolt. He breathed the way he had been taught to breathe when nothing was happening yet: slow, through the nose, like a man pouring water. The trees kept their own counsel. Somewhere in them, a shape would become a decision, or it would not — and either way, he would still be here when it happened.",
    "notes": "The pack is what separates this frame from every other prone rifleman in the series. Bedroll, rope, pouches — the whole of a man's kit riding his back while his eye is down the sights, and the painterly treatment makes the weight of it read in the curve of his back. Paired with the firing-line frames, this is the quiet one: no muzzle flash, no shouting, just the aim and the trees. A visitor stands in front of it the way the soldier lies in front of the treeline — waiting for the shape to become a decision.",
