    "story": "By the third round, the ground had stopped pretending it was solid. Every thump came up through the soles of their boots and into their teeth, and the mud took each one and kept it. The loader knew the round by weight before he saw it — smooth, cold, heavier than it looked — and dropped it without watching it go. The tube would do the rest. The man on the ground was not hurt. He was listening for the whistle that would tell them where the next one was coming from. It came from the right, high and thin, and then the dirt went up in a column behind them, and came down on their helmets like rain that had forgotten how to be water. The dirt settled. The crew did not.",
    "notes": "The dirt column is the frame's signature — a brown tower rising behind the crew, caught at the exact instant it leaves the ground, and no other frame in the series holds an explosion in quite this shape. Around it, four men in four postures: the loader with his back turned, the listener flat on the mud, the two riflemen watching the same sky. Paired with the firing-line frames, this is the crew at work, mud and all. A visitor finds the column first, then counts the men, and then stays to count them again.",
