    "story": "The flame lasted a second. The smoke stayed longer, hanging over the field in a slow white sheet, and the gunner breathed it in without noticing, without a choice. Under the smoke, the tank ticked as it cooled, metal settling against metal, and the air tasted of burnt powder and hot steel. Beyond the smoke, the field went on being a field, and gave no sign the flame had ever passed. Somewhere in it, the round had landed. The gunner did not watch for the landing. The men with the glasses did that. He had the next round to think about. When would the smoke clear enough to see what the flame had done?",
    "notes": "The flame is the longest in the tank group — a horizontal tongue of orange that runs the full width of the frame, and the painterly treatment keeps the flame's shape sharp against the smoke behind it. Paired with the other tank-firing frames, this is the side profile: the whole machine in one line, gunner in the hatch, number and nose art reading like a ship's markings. A visitor follows the flame back to the muzzle, then to the gunner, and then stays to read the markings.",
