    "story": "He was not a soldier. The cap gave him away before anything else did. It was the cap he had worn to the market, and it had no business on a battlefield — which was exactly why it was there. The gun was new to his hands, and his hands kept telling him so. Borrowed shapes do not fit. The stone wall had been there long before him, and would be there long after, and the wall did not care which side of it he lay on. All he knew was the weight of the thing, the smell of oil on it, and the sound of his own breathing, too loud in his ears. Somewhere to the left, the reason for the aiming waited — or did not. He did not move. Moving would have made it real.",
    "notes": "No helmet in this frame — only a soft cloth cap where every other fighter in the series wears steel, and that one difference turns a war photograph into a portrait of a civilian who picked up a gun. Paired with the resistance frames, this is the quietest of them: one man, one wall, one direction of aim, and no enemy in sight. A visitor looks at the cap first and then understands the gun.",
