    "story": "The rifle stood upright in the grass, butt down, barrel to the sky — a staff, not a weapon, for the minute it took him to find his voice. The shout came out of him before he had chosen it. It went up over the wall, over the grass, toward men he could not see, and it carried the word that mattered: medic. He said it again, louder, and this time he heard his own voice the way the wall must have heard it — raw, and younger than the moment asked for. Below him, the fallen man's chest rose once, shallow, and fell. 'Medic.'",
    "notes": "Among the casualty frames, this is the one that shouts — and the shout is the whole point of owning it. The painterly treatment keeps the anguish in the face without turning it into a poster, and the rifle planted upright in the grass reads as a staff rather than a weapon. Paired with the carrying frames, this one supplies the sound the others are silent about.",
