    "story": "The hand made a trumpet of itself. Cupped around the mouth, it took the shout and threw it forward, into the still air, toward men who had not arrived yet and had to. It was not a cry. A cry goes nowhere. A summons goes somewhere, and this one had a direction and a job: bring the stretcher, bring the hands, bring four men where one man's arms would never be enough. Behind him, the man on the ground said nothing. He did not have to. The shout was saying it for him.",
    "notes": "The frame shouts at its owner. Where the companion casualty frame shows the call in profile, here the man turns toward the viewer, hand cupped to his mouth, and the shout leaves the picture altogether — it is aimed past the wall, past the room, at whoever is standing in front of it. Paired with the profile frame, the two are the same call heard twice: once from the side, once head-on. A collector hangs this one where the room is quietest, so the shout has somewhere to land.",
