    "story": "The water came up to their knees and stayed there, cold through the wool, patient as a debt. Each step took a second longer than the step before, because the sea does not give ground the way land does — it holds a man's legs and argues. The craft waited with its ramp down, a door standing open in the middle of the water, and the number on its bow meant nothing to the men wading toward it. Numbers belonged to the navy. The flag belonged to everyone, and it flew above the water, dry and unhurried, while everything below it waded. The tide was coming in, and it did not ask permission.",
    "notes": "A collector pays for the flag here — the single dry, flying thing in a frame full of wet wool and wading men, and the painterly treatment lets the red and white read against the grey water without shouting. Paired with the landing-craft frames, this gives the wide exterior: the craft from outside, the water at knee height, the men in between. With the rail scenes, it gives a collector the landing from both sides of the ramp.",
