    "story": "The steel was the first dry thing he had touched since the shore, cold under his palm, slick with spray. Under his boot, the craft moved — a small shift, a live thing noticing his weight — and for a second he was neither in the water nor on the boat, just between, with one foot in each. The rope hung loose, and nobody had reached for it. Above him, the man already aboard had his arm on the steel. Not a hand held out — a man keeping his own balance while another man found his.",
    "notes": "A collector buys this one for the boot on the ramp. Not the craft, not the flag, not the wide water — the single step where a man stops being in the sea and starts being aboard, water still at his ankles, a hand on the steel. Beside the wide exterior, it works as the close step: the rope hanging loose, the man already aboard keeping his own balance, the climb still happening. A visitor follows the boot to the hand, and the hand to the man aboard, and the landing suddenly has a direction.",
