    "story": "It rode in a man's hand, not on a pole, and it went where the hand went — forward, into the dust, into the tall grass, into whatever the field was hiding. Poles stay behind, planted where the fighting was. The cheer that came out of him had no words in it yet — only the sound of having arrived. The dust rose under their boots and hung in the air like a curtain nobody had bothered to close. Somewhere ahead, the field ended. Not at a wall, not at a line — at a place where the cheer would run out and the shooting would start. The flag kept going. It had somewhere to be.",
    "notes": "No flag in the series comes larger than this one — carried by hand, not planted, and the painterly treatment keeps the red cross sharp against the dust and the grass. Paired with the commando frames, this carries the triumph: berets, rifles carried easy, a cheer with no words in it, and the flag going forward instead of standing still. A visitor reads the flag as a direction, not a decoration, and follows it across the wall.",
