    "story": "The ground did not want to give him up. Every pull took a little of the dirt with it, and the dirt, being dirt, came back for more — under the boots, under the body, into the cuffs of a uniform that had stopped caring what was on it. The dragger held the man's arms and nothing else. Arms were what was offered, so arms were what he took, and the pull came through them and settled somewhere further back, behind the ribs, where hands could not reach it. To the right, the third man lay flat with his rifle and did not move it. That was why the dragger stayed low. The bushes were not far now. The ground let go of him one handful of dirt at a time.",
    "notes": "A collector points to the drag first — a man pulled across open ground by the arms, the body limp, the dirt doing half the work of the picture. Paired with the casualty frames, this works the lowest register: no carrying, no standing, just the pull and the ground and the man at right keeping his rifle on the field. A visitor follows the drag with one eye and the prone rifleman with the other. How far would you drag a man on open ground?",
