    "story": "The cross was the reddest thing in the field. Not paint on a sign — paint on a helmet, worn where a man could see it from a distance and know, before anything else, that this one had come to mend. The medic's hands went to the fallen man's chest, pressing, listening, asking the chest to answer. The helmet on the ground lay empty, a bowl with nothing in it — the fallen man's, and a man who cannot lift his head has no use for a helmet. The medic put his ear close. Breathing, there — shallow, uneven, a small argument against the dirt. Above him, the others walked past. The cross stayed where it was. It had work to do.",
    "notes": "The cross does the collecting here — a red mark on a helmet against all the khaki and dirt, and the painterly treatment lets it read from across the room. Paired with the drag frame, it gives the arrival: the pull over, the tending begun, the helmet on the ground already empty. With the drag frame beside it, a collector owns the casualty's arc: the pull, and then the tending.",
